[{"id":31720,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31720\">#1</a> Daniel Schaeppi’s new paper on the <a href=\"http://arxiv.org/abs/1206.2764\">arxiv</a> contains a more conceptual proof (see Appendix A) of the <a href=\"http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/superextensive+site#compatibility_of_sheafification_7\">theorem</a> that stackification with respect to the singleton covers in a superextensive site preserves sheaves for the extensive topology, hence produces stacks for the whole superextensive topology.  Section 5 also contains a proof that at least in one case, and probably more generally, stackification of internal categories is a bicategorical localization at weak equivalences, and hence (by David Roberts’s analogous theorem for anafunctors) produces a bicategory equivalent to that of internal categories and anafunctors.</p>\n<p>(The rest of the paper is about very concrete algebrogeometric objects that I don’t pretend to understand, but Daniel very helpfully pointed me to the parts that I would be interested in.  (-: )</p>","timestamp":1339660578,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]},{"id":31721,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31721\">#2</a> Hmm, I’d better get a wriggle on and write up some stuff. Thanks for pointing this out. I was vaguely interested in reading this, but felt no pressing need.</p>","timestamp":1339661061,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]},{"id":31737,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31737\">#3</a> Vistoli’s notes <a href=\"http://homepage.sns.it/vistoli/descent.pdf\">here</a> have some relevant stuff, in particular lemmas 4.25-4.27</p>","timestamp":1339738758,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]},{"id":31738,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31738\">#4</a> That seems to be only about characterization of stacks in terms of the two generating subtopologies, which is pretty easy.  The tricky bit is to show that <em>stackification</em> with respect to the singleton topology preserves stacks for the extensive topology.</p>","timestamp":1339750785,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]},{"id":31740,"sender_id":43,"sender_full_name":"DavidRoberts","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/fbc2dbfb28f9e9275a4fd4cd109b5a21?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31740\">#5</a> In a slightly unrelated way, what do you think of this idea, which I’m sure is not hugely deep. Let <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> be a superextensive site. Then <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{\\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> has finite (or small) products. Isn’t a sheaf or a stack for the extensive topology just a product-preserving functor on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S^{\\mathrm{op}}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathrm mtight\">op</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span>? This seems to me to be like some vague shadow of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> being dual to a generalisation of  Lawvere theory, and extensive sheaves/stacks being algebras for that theory. Then sheaves for the full topology on <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">S</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0576em;\">S</span></span></span></span> are those algebras which additionally preserve coequalisers. There should probably be some sort of doctrinal interpretation of this.</p>\n<p>Consider the site which is the coproduct completion of Urs’ favourite site <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/CartSp\">CartSp</a>. The opposite of this category looks very much like a theory.</p>","timestamp":1339752578,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]},{"id":31741,"sender_id":10,"sender_full_name":"Mike Shulman","avatar_url":"https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ede34282f405f902e12fa7e6cf336572?d=identicon&version=1","content":"<p><a href=\"#narrow/channel/16-Preprints-.26-Publications/topic/stacks.20on.20superextensive.20sites/near/31741\">#6</a> Indeed, the opposite of a site is just a particular kind of limit <a href=\"https://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/sketch\">sketch</a>.</p>","timestamp":1339758348,"subject":"stacks on superextensive sites","stream_id":16,"display_recipient":"Preprints & Publications","reactions":[]}]